"ToolBrain - Flexible Reinforcement Learning Framework for Agentic Systems (https://toolbrain.org/get-started/introduction)\nciteturn101search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; RL for Agentic SystemsToolBrain is a lightweight framework that lets you train your existing tool-using agents with RL, using a powerful and intuitive API. ...     from smolagents import CodeAgent, TransformersModel, tool ... Automatically select relevant tools with Tool Retrieval and generate new training tasks with Zero-Learn.\n\n# ToolBrain\n\nRL for Agentic Systems\n\nToolBrain is a lightweight framework that lets you train your existing tool-using agents with RL, using a powerful and intuitive API.\n\n## Get Started in Minutes\n\nThe code below is a complete, runnable example. It trains a simple agent to use a multiply tool. Copy, paste, and run it to see ToolBrain in action.\n\nquickstart_example.py\n    \n    `# 1. Imports and Component Definition\n    from toolbrain import Brain\n    from toolbrain.rewards import reward_exact_match\n    from smolagents import CodeAgent, TransformersModel, tool\n    \n    @tool\n    def multiply(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Multiply two integers.\n    \n        Args:\n            a (int): First factor.\n            b (int): Second factor.\n    \n        Returns:\n            int: Product of a and b.\n        \"\"\"\n        return a * b\n    \n    # Define a standard agent (CPU-compatible)\n    model = TransformersModel(\"Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct\", max_new_tokens=128)\n    agent = CodeAgent(model=model, tools=[multiply], max_steps=1)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - lian-tian-mo-zun/ToolSelf: ToolSelf: Unifying Task Execution and Configuration Generation for Tool-Use Agents · GitHub (https://github.com/lian-tian-mo-zun/ToolSelf)\nciteturn101search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; A tool-use agent framework that lets agents reconfigure themselves while solving the task. ... This repository includes the ToolSelf benchmark runner, a ReAct-style execution agent, web/search/file/code tools, reconfiguration and termination tools, GAIA-style evaluation scripts, benchmark config templates, and result summarization utilities. ...   * Long-horizon focus: designed for deep research, general assistance, and software engineering workflows. ... The paper also introduces Configuration-Aware Two-stage Training (CAT): rejection sampling fine-tuning for cold-start trajectories, followed by trajectory-level KTO reinforcement learning to improve runtime adaptation.\n\n# ToolSelf: Unifying Task Execution and Self-Reconfiguration via Tool-Driven Emergent Adaptation\n\nA tool-use agent framework that lets agents reconfigure themselves while solving the task.\n\nNews · Overview · Method · Quick Start · Datasets · Citation\n\nToolSelf is a tool-use agent framework that unifies task execution and configuration generation in one iterative loop. Instead of fixing the agent configuration before execution, ToolSelf lets the agent update its sub-goals, strategy, toolbox, context-management mode, and inter-stage knowledge at runtime.\n\nThis repository includes the ToolSelf benchmark runner, a ReAct-style execution agent, web/search/file/code tools, reconfiguration and termination tools, GAIA-style evaluation scripts, benchmark config templates, and result summarization utilities.\n\n## News\n\n  * 2026-06-15: ToolSelf code, evaluation runners, config templates, and documentation are released.\n  * 2026-05-31: ToolSelf v3 is available on arXiv: arXiv:2602.07883.\n\n## Overview\n\nMost agent systems choose a configuration before execution: a task decomposition, a toolset, a prompting strategy, and a context policy. ToolSelf makes that configuration a first-class, tool-updatable object. During solving, the agent can call a reconfiguration tool, summarize the current stage, and generate the next configuration before continuing.\n\nOverview of ToolSelf. Configuration becomes a dynamic variable that can be updated through tool calls during execution.\n\n## Highlights\n\n  * Runtime self-reconfiguration: update sub-goals, strategy, tools, task knowledge, and context mode while solving.\n  * One policy, one action space: task execution and adaptation happen inside the same ReAct-style loop.\n  * Tool-native adaptation: reconfiguration is represented as a standard tool call, making it traceable and evaluable.\n  * Long-horizon focus: designed for deep research, general assistance, and software engineering workflows.\n  * Reproducible evaluation: includes isolated benchmark runners, config templates, and result summaries.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAgentTune: A Unified Training Lifecycle for Tool-Using LLM Agents (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13335)\nciteturn101academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 days ago; Title: AgentTune: A Unified Training Lifecycle for Tool-Using LLM Agents\nTitle: AgentTune: A Unified Training Lifecycle for Tool-Using LLM Agents\nAuthors: R E Zera Marveen Lyngkhoi, Abhivansh Gupta, Vidushee Vats, Ram Mohan Rao Kadiyala, Vinay Kumar Sankarapu, Pratinav Seth\nDate: Fri Oct  9 10:09:27 2026\n\nTool-using agents increasingly require a common substrate for building, training, evaluating, distilling, and maintaining agentic systems, yet existing tooling typically addresses these stages in isolation. We present AgentTune, a source-available Python library that provides a unified framework for developing and optimizing tool-using agents. At its core, AgentTune introduces a two-tier EventLog representation that preserves agent trajectories while supporting consistent projections for training, evaluation, and auditing. Building on this substrate, the library provides a shared agent runtime, tool-calling reinforcement learning, agentic distillation, retrieval-augmented generation, decision workflows, evaluation utilities, and a designed path toward self-healing agents. These components are exposed through composable interfaces so that agent strategies, training methods, evaluation procedures, and recovery mechanisms can be combined without requiring separate representations or pipelines. We demonstrate the framework across agentic tasks including legal contract search and domain-specific applications, illustrating its use for trajectory-based optimization, tool-use evaluation, and cross-task agent development. AgentTune is released as a source-available library at https://github.com/Lexsi-Labs/AgentTune--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - ToolBrain/ToolBrain: A framework for agentic tool use training with reinforcement learning · GitHub (https://github.com/ToolBrain/ToolBrain)\nciteturn101search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; ToolBrain is a lightweight open-source Python library for training agentic systems with effective tool usage and built-in reinforcement learning. ... This script demonstrates simplified ToolBrain API: ... If you are using an agent framework not yet supported, we encourage you to build an adapter for it. ... agentic-ai dpo fine-tuning grpo langchain llm-agents reinforcement-learning smolagents tool-use unsloth\n\n# ToolBrain 🧠\n\n### RL Framework for Agentic Tools\n\nToolBrain is a lightweight open-source Python library for training agentic systems with effective tool usage and built-in reinforcement learning.\n📚 Website: toolbrain.org\n📖 Documentation: Read the Docs\n\nSupport us by giving ToolBrain a ⭐ on GitHub.\n\n## ✨ Key Features\n\n  * 🤖 Learning algorithms: Supports GRPO, DPO, and supervised learning.\n  * 🎯 Flexible rewards: Define your own reward functions or use LLM-as-judge.\n  * 🔧 Tool management: Scalable retrieval for managing large tool collections.\n  * 📊 Knowledge distillation: Distill large teacher models into smaller student models for efficiency.\n  * 🚀 Zero-learn: Automatically generate training tasks.\n  * ⚡ Efficient training: Supports FP16 finetuning, LoRA, Unsloth, and BitsAndBytes for resource-efficient training.\n  * 🧠 Multiple agent frameworks: Supports SmolAgent and LangChain, with more coming soon.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSMITH: Joint Optimization of Tool Creation and Use for LLM Agents (https://tool-use-smith.github.io/)\nciteturn101search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # Joint Optimization of Tool Creation and Use for Large Language Model Agents ... ^{1}Appier AI Research   ^{2}National Taiwan University ... SMITH is a training loop, not a fixed pipeline: the same policy invents a tool, uses it to solve problems, and is optimized on whether that use succeeds. ... It also reaches 40.4 on TabMWP-Hard and 42.6 on out-of-domain GQA (+7.6 over the best same-backbone inference-time baseline), without any visual or tabular training data. ... 42.9 held-out accuracy for a 350M model using SMITH's tools ... When the right tool doesn't exist, the agent is stuck. ... The policy sees N = 4 question–answer pairs and must infer the general procedure behind them, then express it as an OpenAI-compatible `(Python function, JSON schema)` pair.\n\nAccepted to NeurIPS 2026 (Poster)\n\n# Joint Optimization of Tool Creation and Use for Large Language Model Agents\n\nSMITH: Schema-grounded Multi-task Iterative Tool Honing\n\n  * Zhi Rui Tam^{1,2}\n  * Chieh-Yen Lin^{1}\n  * Yun-Nung (Vivian) Chen^{2}\n  * Shao-Hua Sun^{1,2}\n  * Hung-yi Lee^{2}\n\n^{1}Appier AI Research   ^{2}National Taiwan University\n\nRead the Paper View Code\n\nImage: SMITH training loop: the same model invents a tool, uses it to solve problems, learns from the results, and gets better. SMITH is a training loop, not a fixed pipeline: the same policy invents a tool, uses it to solve problems, and is optimized on whether that use succeeds. That feedback is what keeps tool creation improving over time.\n\n## Abstract\n\nTool-augmented language models are bounded by the APIs humans bothered to write; existing tool-creation systems patch this by prompting a frozen LLM at inference time, leaving the model that writes a tool decoupled from the one that uses it, with no signal that the schemas it produces are schemas it can actually invoke. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTraining Environments for Interactive AI Agents - Advanced AI Training (https://academy.farvision.com/advanced/training-environments-for-interactive-ai-agents)\nciteturn101search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Design simulation and evaluation environments where AI agents can practice tool use, web navigation, planning, recovery, and long-horizon tasks safely. ...   * Define the components of an environment for web, software, or tool-using agents. ... Training and evaluating in environments exposes the agent to feedback loops. ... For a web-navigation agent, observations may include page text, screenshots, DOM elements, and previous actions.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPaper page - CoVe: Training Interactive Tool-Use Agents via Constraint-Guided Verification (https://huggingface.co/papers/2603.01940)\nciteturn101search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: last month; CoVe is a post-training data synthesis framework that generates high-quality training trajectories for interactive tool-use agents by incorporating task constraints as verification mechanisms, achieving superior performance compared to baseline models. ... To address this gap, we introduce CoVe (Constraint-Verification), a post-training data synthesis framework designed for training interactive tool-use agents while ensuring both data complexity and correctness. ... Get this paper in your agent:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTool-Use RL Training — Beag Labs — Beag Labs (https://www.beaglabs.com/tra